15.1  rwx, octal and umask

TL;DR
  - Every file has an owner, a group, and three sets of rights: for the owner,
    for the group, for everyone else.
  - r read, w write, x execute. On a folder, x means "may enter".
  - In octal, r=4, w=2, x=1, added per set: 644 is rw-r--r--, 755 is rwxr-xr-x.
  - The umask removes rights from new files: with 022, files get 644 and folders
    755.

      ls -l notes.txt
      chmod 640 notes.txt     # owner rw, group r, others nothing
      chmod u+x deploy.sh     # let the owner run it

On your machine
  User      alice (uid 1000, gid 1000)
  Groups    alice, wheel, docker, 4242
  Admin     member of wheel: the group sudo usually trusts
  Umask     0022: new files 644 (rw-r--r--), new folders 755

Pitfalls
  - On a folder, r lists the names and x lets you open what's inside: with r but
    no x, you see names you can't open.
  - Deleting a file depends on the folder's w, not on the file's rights.
  - chmod -R 777 "fixes" an error by letting everyone write everything. Fix the
    owner or the group instead.
  - Root ignores read and write rights, but still needs some x bit to run a
    file.
  - The umask never adds rights: new files start from 666, folders from 777, so
    a new file is never executable.

Deep dive

  ▾ Why octal?

    Each set of rights is three bits, and one octal digit is exactly three bits,
    so 750 reads as owner 7 (rwx), group 5 (r-x), others 0. A fourth digit in
    front holds the setuid, setgid and sticky bits (see 15.2):
    chmod 2775 shared/.

  ▾ Where does the umask come from?

    A process inherits it from its parent. Your login sets it, from
    /etc/login.defs or a shell startup file, and you can change it for the
    current shell with umask 077. systemd services use the UMask= setting of
    their unit, 0022 by default.

    Common values are 022 (others can read) and 077 (private). Some distros use
    002 for regular users, since each user has a private group of their own.

Related
  Commands  chmod, chown, umask
  See also  idk 15.2 setuid, setgid and sticky bit
  See also  idk 15.3 sudo and groups
